---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-smsbump';
const title = "Postscript vs SMSBump for Shopify: Migration After Retirement";
const desc = "Postscript vs retired SMSBump: Shopify SMS recipient migration, reply and queue ownership, continuing Yotpo modules, and replacement economics.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is SMSBump still a current alternative to Postscript?",
    "answer": "No. Yotpo has decommissioned SMS and announced full Email/SMS deprecation. Compare an active replacement sender while deciding separately whether to retain Reviews and Loyalty."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Postscript replace the entire old stack?",
    "answer": "Do not assume that. Validate SMS imports, number and reply handoff, and each required email workflow. Confirm email rollout access for the account before replacing an existing ESP; Reviews and Loyalty are separate jobs."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare pricing?",
    "answer": "Quote the same eligible recipients, countries, message segments and peak calendar. Include carrier charges, minimums, transfer, connectors and labor, with continuing module costs shown separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does keeping Yotpo Loyalty eliminate integration work?",
    "answer": "No. Define customer identifiers, tier events, freshness and replay handling, then confirm the actual connector and owner. A shared business workflow does not prove a middleware-free connection."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do I need a separate email supplier?",
    "answer": "Keep a confirmed email sender until Postscript account access and required email workflows are demonstrated. There is no automatic reason to replace it with Sequenzy or another provider just to move SMS."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which compliance tooling wins?",
    "answer": "This page establishes no ranking or legal conclusion. Require current controls for signup evidence, imports, opt-outs and quiet hours, and assign review for each destination and purpose."
  },
  {
    "question": "What decides cart-recovery readiness?",
    "answer": "Use a purchase arriving after recovery is queued. Verify cancellation in every active sender, current channel eligibility and replay deduplication, with a trace for the test recipient."
  },
  {
    "question": "What if old records cannot be exported?",
    "answer": "Do not assume the retired dashboard is accessible. Inventory saved records, document missing evidence and obtain supported recovery guidance; keep unsupported records suppressed rather than inventing permission."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Postscript vs SMSBump</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs SMSBump for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">SMSBump is a retired source system, not a current purchase choice. Evaluate Postscript for the text workloads you can substantiate, while separating recipient migration, continuing Yotpo modules and email ownership.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Active text candidate", "tool": "Postscript", "reason": "Demonstrate SMS workflows and reply ownership."}, {"label": "Historical source", "tool": "SMSBump", "reason": "Reconcile saved records; messaging is decommissioned."}, {"label": "Continuing modules", "tool": "Yotpo Reviews / Loyalty", "reason": "Retain content and balances on their own requirements."}, {"label": "Email scope", "tool": "Confirm account access", "reason": "Postscript markets email; rollout access needs confirmation."}]} />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Postscript</th><th>SMSBump (Yotpo SMS)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody><tr><td>Status</td><td>Active SMS/RCS offering</td><td>Messaging decommissioned</td></tr><tr><td>Recipients</td><td>Receiving import requirements must pass</td><td>Saved evidence requires reconciliation</td></tr><tr><td>Replies</td><td>Demonstrate inbox and number handoff</td><td>Do not assume dashboard access</td></tr><tr><td>Loyalty</td><td>Confirm event connection and owner</td><td>Continuing module; not an SMS sender</td></tr><tr><td>Email</td><td>Confirm rollout access and workflow scope</td><td>Email deprecated; no separate fallback</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Messaging retirement changes the decision</h2>
<p>As checked November 25, 2026, Yotpo’s <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">decommission notice</a> says the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. SMSBump is historical naming here. Yotpo Email is not a separately available fallback. Reviews and Loyalty remain separate continuing products; keeping them does not restore messaging. These proposed exercises report no merchant deployment, measured lift or completed migration.</p><h2>Postscript’s current scope and the email boundary</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/">Postscript</a> markets SMS and RCS alongside email. Its <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">email page</a> offers signup for access, so treat rollout and account eligibility as procurement questions rather than assuming every SMS account can replace an email sender today. Obtain written access, supported workflows and launch dates before retiring the current ESP. SMS campaign and reply workflows can be evaluated without making that email change.</p>
<p>The potential benefit is one operational home for eligible text campaigns and customer replies. Demonstrate that benefit with an assigned reply inbox, coverage hours and escalation path. RCS branding and rich content are a separate acceptance item: verify carrier/device coverage, approval and SMS fallback before budgeting reach. Neither channel breadth nor shared vendor branding proves better delivery or stronger compliance than another active provider.</p>
<h2>Recipient handoff before a vinyl drop</h2>
<p>For a proposed 300-unit vinyl pressing, build a migration ledger from saved exports and available permission records. Preserve normalized phone, country, signup source, timestamp, disclosure version, opt-out history and the source identifier. Reconcile duplicate numbers and conflicting statuses with suppression taking precedence; an email subscriber or loyalty member is not automatically eligible for SMS. Quarantine records lacking evidence instead of importing them as subscribed.</p>
<p>Obtain the receiving provider’s written import and sender-transfer requirements. Determine whether an existing number can move, what happens to keywords and inbound replies, and who handles messages during the transition. Do not promise access to a decommissioned dashboard or exports. If evidence is unavailable, document the gap and the supported recovery or fresh signup route. Use staff-owned test recipients to verify keyword routing and opt-out propagation before a drop; never reactivate the retired queue as a fallback.</p>
<h2>Board-game allocation: 1,050 orders and $67k per month</h2>
<p>This proposed baseline centers preorder allocation before Essen. Give each allocation, purchase and fulfillment event an order identifier, timestamp and revision. A late allocation event must not enroll an already purchased order in discount recovery. Pause winback until the chosen fulfillment state is satisfied; test partial shipment separately from fully shipped.</p>
<p>Inventory a pending recovery, allocation notice and broadcast for the same test recipient. Name one owner for each purpose, disable duplicate enrollment in Shopify and other active apps, cancel obsolete jobs, then replay the old allocation event. Acceptance requires a trace showing why each remaining message is eligible, not merely a quiet inbox. A forty-five-minute delay is a proposed race-condition input, not a safe universal exclusion window.</p>
<h2>Tea and coffee require different subscription exits</h2>
<p>For tea, retain the proposed 720 orders/$44k monthly baseline and 28–35 day tin-consumption window. Model blend, gift recipient and subscription pause independently. An email gift receipt must not create SMS permission. Test a skip received after enqueueing: the promotional replenishment must exit, while any separately authorized subscription notice follows its own owner and purpose. Compare two consumption windows in a planned holdout; neither interval is an established optimum.</p>
<p>For coffee, use 860 orders/$52k monthly and a proposed day-thirty reorder tied to roast date. Split wholesale sample follow-up from retail replenishment, and send a pause event with a later timestamp than the roast event. Require the final eligibility check to honor that pause even after a connector reconnect. Measure editing, exception handling and reply handoff time if reduced operator effort is a goal; no hours saved are claimed.</p>
<h2>Climbing, camping and pottery: acceptance at the boundary</h2>
<p>Climbing’s proposed 1,100 orders/$89k monthly fixture uses seven-to-fourteen day harness research. Keep fit-page interest separate from product suitability or certification. Link education to manufacturer documentation; a purchase must cancel obsolete browse prompts. Assign safety questions to trained support rather than treating a text reply as product approval.</p>
<p>Camping’s proposed 1,350 orders/$78k monthly fixture uses tent-capacity quiz preferences. Preserve quiz version and chosen capacity, then test a mobile browse-to-cart transition, stock change and subsequent purchase. Quiz participation alone cannot subscribe the recipient. Require a defined fallback when capacity is absent instead of sending an arbitrary tent offer.</p>
<p>Pottery’s proposed 480 orders/$31k monthly fixture separates class waitlists from glaze-kit promotions. Import source and purpose independently. A staff test record requesting a class seat must stay outside product broadcasts unless separately eligible. Reconcile that record through unsubscribe, reimport and replay to catch accidental resubscription.</p>
<h2>Retaining Yotpo modules without buying retired messaging</h2>
<p>A jewelry VIP-tier exercise can retain Loyalty while evaluating Postscript for eligible texts. Define the customer identifier, tier and points fields, event freshness limit, connector owner and replay key. Show a tier upgrade followed by a downgrade before send time. If freshness fails, omit the points claim or hold the promotion. Price connector maintenance explicitly; this is not a promise of native delivery without middleware.</p>
<p>Reviews can still supply useful product content where licensed and supported. Verify product/variant mapping and reuse rights independently of the SMS transfer. Keeping familiar review widgets or reward balances may reduce module replacement work, but does not establish the new sender’s event contract. Keep email newsletters with a sender whose access and workflows are confirmed; editorial staff picks and coffee stories remain useful jobs even when the SMS owner changes.</p>
<h2>Procurement and fixed-goods-cost economics</h2>
<p>Use 12,000–18,000 contacts only as a proposed quoting range. Count eligible SMS recipients separately, then quote the same countries, segment lengths, peak sends, carrier charges, minimums, support and annual commitment. Separate continuing Reviews/Loyalty fees from the replacement sender, number transfer, connector work and staff labor. There is no current SMSBump renewal quote to compare as an active messaging option.</p>
<p>For an illustrative $1,000 sale with $400 fixed goods cost and $100 discount, contribution before other costs is $500: $1,000 − $100 − $400. Do not apply a margin percentage to discounted revenue while also claiming goods cost is fixed. With $20 incremental messaging expense, the remainder is $480 before other variable costs. Estimate incremental orders with a planned randomized holdout, including uncertainty, rather than equating attributed revenue with migration payback.</p>
<h2>Accept the handoff, then decide on scope</h2>
<p>Approve Postscript for the demonstrated SMS workload only after recipient reconciliation, number/reply ownership, cancellation and replay checks pass and the quote matches the calendar. Hold unsupported imports or unavailable channels outside that scope. Retain or replace Reviews/Loyalty on their own requirements. The useful decision is whether the active sender can operate the defined jobs, with a documented incident owner and fallback, not whether a retired Yotpo bundle once looked cheaper. See the <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing guide</a> for the procurement worksheet.</p>
</article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Klaviyo vs Yotpo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/recart-vs-postscript">Recart vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/jewelry">Email tools for jewelry stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs SMSBump FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
